  "source": "Hand calculation from statute, by W5 (Claude Code, for Max Ghenis), 20 October 2026. A single adult with £10,000 of employment income in every year, no children, no savings, dividend or property income, no salary sacrifice, and no fuel or rail spending. Seven of the eight measures change nothing for them in any year: income tax is nil below the personal allowance, frozen (£12,570) or uprated, so the threshold freeze changes nothing; the dividend, savings and property rate rises apply to income they don't have; the two-child limit to children; and the fuel duty and rail fares freezes to spending, which is nil. So each change is £0. The salary sacrifice cap is left out, and so is the total: policyengine-uk models employers spreading the cap's extra National Insurance over all workers' pay (salary_sacrifice_broad_base_haircut, at 0.16% while the cap applies), a modelling choice that moves this household's income, not a rule of statute. This checks that the route answers and that no other measure reaches a household it can't affect; it is not a check of a non-zero answer."
